The formula 
	 P \propto \rho^{5/3} 
	 describes a proportional relationship between Standard Stiffness (
	 P 
	 ) and the material's density (
	 \rho 
	 ). The exponent 5/3 indicates that as the density increases, the stiffness increases at a more rapid rate. This relationship often arises in systems governed by Fermi-Dirac statistics or in situations where pressure is a result of quantum mechanical effects, such as electron degeneracy pressure in white dwarf stars. The constant of proportionality (implied by the α symbol) would encapsulate other relevant physical parameters not explicitly shown in this simplified form.
